<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RequestedTheme is pinned Dark here, and the pin is what makes Palette.xaml's 「Dark」 dictionary the one
  an application-scope lookup lands in. The property can only be written before content loads, so it
  cannot be the switch; what it is instead is a fixed answer to 「which ThemeDictionary does an
  application-scope lookup use」. Two readers resolve at application scope on purpose —
  DiagnosticsViewModel.Resolve and PlayerPage.BrushFor — and with this pinned they always read the same
  dictionary, whatever the Windows app mode is. Unpinned they followed the app mode: on a light-mode
  machine anything resolved outside the tree came back in light ink and got drawn on a near-black
  surface. That is not hypothetical: ShellSelfCheck's palette check exited non-zero on a light-mode
  machine with nothing wrong with it, and DiagnosticsViewModel's log levels came out light-on-dark for
  the same reason.

  The pin got *more* load-bearing on 2026-09-05, when that dictionary's key changed from 「Default」 to
  「Dark」 to satisfy winui-design's 「Light, Dark and HighContrast explicitly — never Default」. There is no
  Default left to fall back to, so if this line ever stops taking effect the lookup no longer quietly
  finds the dark palette anyway — it misses and gets the framework's own value. The self-check's 「应用主题」
  line is the tripwire for that.

  What actually changes the theme is Theme/ThemeHost.cs. It writes the chosen theme's colours into the
  SolidColorBrush objects of *both* painted ThemeDictionaries — Dark and Light — and sets ElementTheme on
  the registered roots to match, so the framework's own hundreds of unoverridden brushes flip with it.
  Writing both is what keeps those two app-scope readers agreeing with the trees they draw into: whatever
  dictionary a lookup lands in, it holds the same theme. All five themes in UiThemes are dark today
  (daylight was deleted 2026-09-05), so Light is currently painted and never selected — kept because
  dropping it would take the light half of the derivation with it.

  Palette.xaml is merged after XamlControlsResources so its keys shadow the framework's. Styles.xaml comes
  after Palette.xaml, and that order is load-bearing: Palette.xaml holds only colour now, Styles.xaml holds
  the type, the geometry and the reusable shapes, and one of its styles reaches back for a colour key.
-->
<Application
    x:Class="EmbyNian.Shell.App"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    RequestedTheme="Dark">
    <Application.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <XamlControlsResources xmlns="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="ms-appx:///Theme/Palette.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="ms-appx:///Theme/Styles.xaml" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Application.Resources>
</Application>
